consiSt("mp",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"NMI Supreme Court (Commonwealth Supreme Court)","d":"A chief justice and at least two associate justices; appeals are decided by three justices. Hears appeals from judgments and orders of the Superior Court, issues writs, answers certified questions from federal courts and from Commonwealth officials, has appellate jurisdiction over attorney discipline, and controls bar admission and court rules. It cannot take new evidence and reviews findings of fact only for clear error. Its decisions are final except as Covenant § 403(a) provides."},{"l":"TRIAL COURT","n":"Commonwealth Superior Court","d":"The single general-jurisdiction trial court for all civil (law and equity) and criminal cases. A presiding judge and at least four associate judges; at least one full-time judge must be assigned to Rota and one to Tinian. The Family Court and the Drug Court are divisions within it. There is no intermediate appellate court."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Small claims (within the Superior Court)","d":"Small claims are heard in the Superior Court. The Judiciary Fee Schedule has small-claims filing tiers up to $5,000, and the civil rules say former Commonwealth Rule 83 governs small claims until separate Small Claims Rules are adopted. The jurisdictional dollar limit was not confirmed from the collected texts."},{"l":"FEDERAL (SEPARATE)","n":"U.S. District Court for the Northern Mariana Islands","d":"A federal territorial court, separate from the Commonwealth courts and covered elsewhere in Consi. Its unpublished trial and appellate division opinions may be cited in the Superior Court under NMI R. Civ. P. 82.2(a). This page covers only the Commonwealth courts."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Commonwealth Code","d":"The CNMI has no anti-SLAPP statute that could be found. Searches of the Law Revision Commission’s official Commonwealth Code (by section title, for “SLAPP,” “strategic lawsuit,” “public participation,” and “special motion”) and its site-wide search across public laws, the Code, and the Commonwealth Register found no anti-SLAPP law. The only site-wide “SLAPP” hit was the word “slapping” in a 2025 child-care regulation. Title 7 (Civil Procedure) has no chapter on the subject. There is no special motion to strike, discovery stay, or fee-shifting rule tied to speech or petitioning in the collected civil or appellate rules.\nRelated speech-tort rules that do exist: the Uniform Single Publication Act (one cause of action per publication, 7 CMC § 2411), and a 2-year limitation for slander (7 CMC § 2503(a)). Defend a speech-based suit with ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) and Rule 56 motions and Rule 11 sanctions.\nA law passed recently but not yet codified would not show up in these searches. Check the Legislature’s site (cnmileg.net) for any pending or recent bill before advising a client.","c":"None found; checked Commonwealth Code (cnmilaw.gov), 7 CMC Divs. 1–4; see 7 CMC §§ 2411, 2503","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: who is eligible","d":"To appear pro hac vice, the lawyer must:\n1. practice law outside the Commonwealth;\n2. be in good standing everywhere admitted;\n3. associate with a local attorney in good standing;\n4. not be admitted in the Commonwealth;\n5. not keep an office in the Commonwealth;\n6. never have been denied admission to the bar; and\n7. not live in the Commonwealth.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(a) (Rules of Admission, eff. Aug. 4, 2025)","v":true},{"t":"Apply to the Supreme Court, not the trial judge; $5,000 fee","d":"File the application with the Commonwealth Supreme Court. It must show that you meet Rule 73-1, give the name and business address of the local attorney, and explain why the client needs you in particular in the Commonwealth courts.\nPay $5,000 to the Commonwealth Treasury and attach the receipt, or a check or money order payable to the Treasury. A lawyer representing a Commonwealth or U.S. governmental agency or instrumentality may ask for a waiver in a separate written request attached to the motion.\nAttach a certificate of good standing from every jurisdiction where you are admitted. Also attach a sworn affidavit from the local attorney stating that the local attorney is a member of the Bar in good standing and keeps an office in the Commonwealth.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(b)–(e); Judiciary Fee Schedule item 116 (eff. Mar. 1, 2017)","v":true},{"t":"One new case per calendar year; admission expires after 12 months","d":"You may be admitted pro hac vice in only one new case per calendar year. You cannot apply in a second case until the first case is closed. Unless the order sets a different date, admission expires 12 months after it is granted. To keep going after that, you must file a new application and pay a new $5,000 fee.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(f)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must take part in the case","d":"The civil rules require any off-island counsel to associate with local counsel. Off-island counsel means a lawyer admitted in the Commonwealth who does not regularly keep an office there. Local counsel must keep an office in the Commonwealth and regularly practice in its courts.\nLocal counsel has full authority as attorney of record and must take a meaningful part in preparing and trying the case. Local counsel must also attend all proceedings when needed. Service on local counsel counts as service on off-island counsel unless the judge excuses it for good cause.\nFor a pro hac vice lawyer, Rule 73-1(e) separately requires a local attorney who is a member of the Bar in good standing with a Commonwealth office.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 5(b)(4) (the Rule 5 note calls it 5(b)(3)); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(e)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing registration is mandatory, including for pro hac vice counsel","d":"All attorneys must register with the court’s E-system and use it, including lawyers admitted pro hac vice and lawyers representing themselves. A pro hac vice lawyer must register once admitted and must enter an appearance in the case. Every e-filed document must be signed electronically (for example, “/s/”) and show your name, mailing address, email, phone, and bar number.","c":"NMI R. Elec. Filing 4(a)–(b), 8(a), 8(c) (eff. Dec. 11, 2021)","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice lawyers are subject to CNMI discipline","d":"The attorney discipline rules define “attorney” to include anyone admitted pro hac vice. They also cover suspended lawyers, government attorneys with provisional licenses, and legal interns. You are subject to discipline for conduct inside or outside the Commonwealth.","c":"NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 1(b), 3","v":true},{"t":"Full admission: NextGen UBE, or the Attorney’s Exam for experienced lawyers","d":"Regular applicants take the NextGen UBE plus the Local Content Essays (LCE), which start in July 2026. The passing score is 615 on the NextGen UBE and 26 on the LCE.\nA lawyer licensed in any U.S. state, territory, or possession who has actively practiced for at least 5 of the last 10 years may instead take the Attorney’s Exam. That exam is only the LCE, and the passing score is 26. Attorney applicants in good standing everywhere they are admitted do not need the MPRE.\nFees under the 2017 schedule: $1,000 for an attorney applicant and $500 for a regular applicant, plus NCBE character and fitness fees. Applicants may be admitted provisionally while the character report is pending.\nThe passing-score order is captioned No. 2025-ADM-0006-RUL but was e-filed April 2, 2026 under case no. ADM-2026. The Judiciary’s bar page labels it “2026-ADM-0006-RUL.”","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 71-5(c), 72-1, 72-2, 72-3; Order Setting Passing Score (Apr. 2, 2026); Fee Schedule items 107–110","v":true},{"t":"Government and legal-services lawyers: limited admission","d":"A salaried lawyer for the Commonwealth Government or Micronesian Legal Services Corporation who passed a bar exam and is admitted in another U.S. jurisdiction may get limited admission without an exam. It covers only work for that employer. The term is 4 years, and it may be extended once for 4 more years if you apply at least 30 days before it expires. You must report employment changes within 7 days. You also need 10 CLE credits of Judiciary community outreach on top of the regular CLE requirement. The application fee is $500.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-2(a)–(i); Fee Schedule item 111","v":true},{"t":"Guam reciprocity: listed on the Judiciary site, rule not found","d":"The Judiciary’s Bar Admissions page offers a “Guam Attorney Reciprocity Application” with a $100 application fee. The Rules of Admission effective August 4, 2025 that were collected here do not contain a reciprocity rule. Ask the Bar Administrator about eligibility, and find the governing order, before relying on it.","c":"nmijudiciary.gov/bar-admissions (form list); no rule located in NMI Sup. Ct. R. 70–75","v":false},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances: no rule found","d":"The collected civil rules, Supreme Court rules, and e-filing rules have no limited-scope or unbundled appearance rule. The Superior Court Rules of Practice could not be checked because most of the posted PDF is scanned images with no text layer. Assume an appearance is for the whole case unless the court orders otherwise.","c":"None found in NMI R. Civ. P., NMI Sup. Ct. R., or NMI R. Elec. Filing","v":false},{"t":"Leaving the islands: file an Off-Island Report 15 days ahead","d":"A lawyer who will be away from the Commonwealth, or unavailable at a regular Commonwealth office, for 3 or more consecutive days must file an Off-Island Report. File it through the CNMI Bar Association website at least 15 days before the absence begins.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 82.1","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: the most recent ABA Model Rules, adopted by reference","d":"The CNMI has no separate code of professional conduct of its own. Lawyers may be disciplined for any act or omission that violates “the most recent version” of the ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Legal interns must swear that they will follow the ABA Model Rules. In practice, the current Model Rules and their comments are the text to read.\nBecause the adoption is dynamic, ABA amendments apply automatically, with no local adoption order. That differs from states that adopt a fixed version.","c":"NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 3(1) (eff. Sept. 17, 2019); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-3(a)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Cannabis carve-out","d":"A lawyer may counsel or assist a client about the CNMI’s marijuana and cannabis laws without violating the Model Rules. If CNMI law differs from federal law, the lawyer must also advise the client about federal law and policy.","c":"NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 3(1)","v":true},{"t":"Extra grounds for discipline beyond the Model Rules","d":"Beyond the Model Rules, Rule 3 makes these grounds for discipline:\n• any act of moral turpitude, dishonesty, or corruption, even without a conviction;\n• willful violation of a court order;\n• violating the attorney’s oath or duties;\n• appearing for a party without authority;\n• misstatements in an admission or reinstatement application;\n• failing to self-report discipline imposed anywhere else;\n• practicing with a disbarred or suspended lawyer, including sharing an office or splitting fees with one or with any person not authorized to practice;\n• “repeated acts that evidence a disregard for civility and professionalism.”","c":"NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 3(2)–(9)","v":true},{"t":"Report discipline elsewhere within 15 days","d":"You must notify the NMI Supreme Court of any pending disciplinary action against you in another jurisdiction within 15 days after you learn of it. Identify the jurisdiction and summarize the matter. Then send a copy of the final decision within 15 days after it issues.\nFor reciprocal discipline, the Court issues an order to show cause, and you have 30 days to respond. To avoid it, you must show by clear and convincing evidence that the other proceeding denied due process, that its evidence was clearly insufficient, or that the misconduct deserves substantially different discipline here.","c":"NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 16(a)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"10-year limitation period; hearings in the Superior Court","d":"Discipline must be sought within 10 years after the act is completed. That limit does not apply to criminal-conviction or reciprocal-discipline proceedings, or to conduct the lawyer intentionally concealed.\nThe Bar’s Disciplinary Committee prosecutes, and the Superior Court holds the hearing. The respondent must answer within 20 days after service of the complaint, or the charges are deemed admitted. The hearing is held 30 to 90 days after the answer. Discipline takes effect when the Supreme Court issues an implementing order, and the Supreme Court has appellate jurisdiction over discipline.","c":"NMI R. Att’y Disc. & P. 2, 13(a), (d)–(e), (l)(3); 1 CMC § 3102(c)","v":true}],"style":"Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands","home":"https://www.nmijudiciary.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Post-trial motions: 10 days, not 28","d":"A Rule 59(e) motion to alter or amend is due 10 days after entry of judgment. So are a Rule 50(b) renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law and a Rule 52(b) motion to amend findings. A motion for a new trial is due in 20 days, and the court’s own power to order a new trial ends at 20 days. The federal rule allows 28 days for each.\nThe court cannot extend any of these deadlines. Because 10 days is “10 days or less,” intermediate weekends and holidays are skipped, but do not rely on that cushion.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), 6(a)(1)(D), 6(b)(2)"},{"t":"A late post-judgment motion does not toll the appeal","d":"The 30-day appeal clock restarts only for motions filed “within the time allotted” by the NMI civil rules. A Rule 59(e) motion filed on day 20 under the federal habit is untimely and does not toll. A Rule 60 motion tolls only if filed within 28 days after judgment. The Supreme Court cannot extend the notice-of-appeal deadline except under Rule 4(a)(5)–(6).","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1), 4(a)(4)(A), 26(b)(1)"},{"t":"30 days to appeal, even when the government is a party","d":"File the notice of appeal with the Superior Court clerk, not the Supreme Court, within 30 days after entry. There is no 60-day period for government cases as in federal court. Other parties get 14 days after the first notice, or the regular 30 days if that ends later. Pay the $175 fee when you file.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(a)(1), 3(e), 4(a)(1), 4(a)(3); Fee Schedule item 81"},{"t":"No separate judgment document means a 150-day wait","d":"The notice of appeal must attach the judgment or order being appealed. In a civil case that means a separate entry of judgment. Without a separate document, the judgment is treated as entered only after 150 days have run from its docketing. A premature notice is held until the separate judgment is filed.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(c)(1)(B) & note 1, 4(a)(2), 4(a)(7)(A)(ii)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice is $5,000 and one new case a year","d":"The fee is $5,000 per application, and you may apply in only one new case per calendar year. You cannot seek admission in a second case until the first is closed. Admission lapses after 12 months, and renewing requires a new application and a new fee. Budget for it, and plan for long cases.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 73-1(c), (f); Fee Schedule item 116"},{"t":"Off-island counsel need active local counsel","d":"Even a CNMI-admitted lawyer without a regular Commonwealth office must associate local counsel. Local counsel must take a meaningful part in preparing and trying the case and attend proceedings. Service on local counsel binds you.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 5(b)(4)"},{"t":"Motions must be filed 30 days before the hearing; opposition is due in 9","d":"Do not choose a hearing date yourself; the clerk sets it. Your opposition is due 9 days after the motion is served, which is 9 days excluding weekends and holidays. It is not keyed to the hearing date as in many state courts. A memorandum over 25 pages filed without permission may be struck.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 6(c)(1)–(2), 7(b)(5)–(6)"},{"t":"Jury demand: 60 days, a $400 fee, and 6 unanimous jurors","d":"Serve the jury demand no later than 60 days after the last pleading is filed. Pay the non-refundable $400 jury fee on or before the day the demand is filed; without proper service, filing, and payment, the jury is waived. A proper demand can be withdrawn only by consent and no later than 45 days before trial. The verdict must be unanimous and returned by 6 jurors. Jury trials also require advance deposits: $1,500 for trials of less than 5 business days, plus $500 for each added day.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d), (f), 48(b); Fee Schedule items 48–49"},{"t":"No mail service in the last 10 days before a hearing or trial","d":"Papers about a motion with a set hearing date, or about a matter set for trial, may not be served by mail within 10 days of the hearing or trial date. Use e-service or hand delivery.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 5(b)(2)(C)"},{"t":"Supreme Court docketing statement within 15 days or risk dismissal","d":"Within 15 days after the Clerk gives notice that the appeal is docketed, file the docketing statement (Form 2). Extensions are disfavored, and failing to file is grounds for dismissal. Also within 15 days after filing the notice of appeal, order transcripts on Form 3 or certify that none will be ordered. Opposing counsel must respond or file an appearance within 10 days after being served with the docketing statement.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(a)(4)–(5), 3-1(b), 11-1(b)(1)"},{"t":"Briefs: 35 pages including everything","d":"A principal brief is limited to 35 pages and a reply to 20, “inclusive of the caption, headings, table of contents, etc.” The limit is pages, not words. Briefs use 11-point Times New Roman with 1.5 spacing. A brief that does not conform may be struck, fined, or lead to dismissal. Only one automatic 30-day extension is available per brief.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31-1(a)(1), 32(a)(4)–(7), 32(i)"},{"t":"Slander has a 2-year limit","d":"Actions for slander, assault and battery, false imprisonment, medical malpractice, and wrongful death or personal injury must be filed within 2 years. Most other actions get 6 years. Do not assume a one-year defamation period as in many states, or a three-year one.","c":"7 CMC §§ 2503, 2505"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge; disqualification for cause by statute","d":"The collected statutes and rules give no peremptory change of judge. Disqualification is governed by statute. A justice or judge must step aside whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned. The statute also lists specific grounds:\n• personal bias, or personal knowledge of disputed facts;\n• prior work on the matter as a lawyer, or by a former law partner;\n• government work on the matter;\n• a financial interest, however small, held by the judge or the judge’s spouse or minor child;\n• a party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness within the second degree of relationship.\nParties may waive only the general “impartiality” ground, and only after full disclosure on the record. The listed grounds cannot be waived.","c":"1 CMC § 3308(a)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: self-recusal or a motion stating specific grounds","d":"A judge who sees grounds for disqualification must recuse on the judge’s own initiative and enter a written recusal order. The clerk sends it to any party not present. A party may move to disqualify, stating the grounds specifically. The statute sets no deadline for the motion, so file promptly; case law on timeliness was not checked.","c":"1 CMC § 3309(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court: mandatory notice of potential conflicts","d":"With its first docketing statement, response, or notice of appearance, each party in a Supreme Court appeal must file a notice listing any potential § 3308 conflicts that might keep a justice from sitting, or state that it knows of none. This notice is not a motion to disqualify, and listing a conflict does not prevent a later motion.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(c) & note 2","v":true},{"t":"Pro tem justices and judges may come from outside the CNMI","d":"The Chief Justice may designate an active or former justice or judge of the Commonwealth, of any U.S. jurisdiction, or of the Federated States of Micronesia, Palau, or the Marshall Islands to sit pro tem. Expect visiting jurists when local judges are disqualified.","c":"NMI Const. art. IV, § 9(d)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"NMI Const. art. I, § 2 | Text mirrors the First Amendment","d":"Article I, § 2 bars laws respecting an establishment of religion or abridging free exercise, speech, press, peaceable assembly, or petitioning the government. Its wording tracks the First Amendment. Whether CNMI courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; no case law was collected.","c":"NMI Const. art. I, § 2","v":true},{"t":"Express constitutional right of privacy, with a compelling-interest test","d":"Article I, § 10 states that the right of individual privacy “shall not be infringed except upon a showing of compelling interest.” The Law Revision Commission’s annotation ties it to the invasion-of-privacy action under 7 CMC §§ 2411–2412. Privacy claims therefore sit alongside speech claims.","c":"NMI Const. art. I, § 10","v":true},{"t":"Single-publication rule and a short slander limitation","d":"One edition, broadcast, or exhibition supports only one cause of action for libel, slander, or invasion of privacy, and the recovery covers damages in all jurisdictions. Slander must be sued on within 2 years. Libel is not listed in the 2-year statute, so on the statute’s face it falls under the 6-year catch-all. Confirm with case law before relying on the longer period.","c":"7 CMC §§ 2411, 2503(a), 2505","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Restatements are the default rules of decision","d":"When there is no written law or local customary law, CNMI courts apply the common law as expressed in the American Law Institute’s Restatements. Where the Restatements are silent, they apply the common law as generally understood in the United States.","c":"7 CMC § 3401","v":true},{"t":"Court rules take effect only after the Legislature has its chance to veto","d":"Proposed court rules go to the Legislature and take effect 60 days later unless a majority of either house disapproves. The Legislature may also annul or amend any rule by statute. Each adoption order recites this 60-day period, so check the order’s effective date, not the date it was signed.","c":"NMI Const. art. IV, § 9(a); 1 CMC § 3403(c)","v":true},{"t":"Superior Court motion practice","d":"The filing clerk, not counsel, sets the hearing date. A pre-judgment motion must be filed and served at least 30 days before the hearing. The opposition is due 9 days after service and the reply 6 days after that; both periods are 10 days or less, so weekends and holidays are skipped. Memoranda are limited to 25 pages, and replies to 15. A memorandum is required when a motion turns on interpreting the law. At the hearing, the movant gets 15 minutes, the opponent 15, and the movant 5 for reply.\nThe court may decide a motion without a hearing after giving the parties 2 days to object. Post-judgment motions run on 14/7/2 calendar days.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 6(c)(1)–(3), 7(b)(4)–(7), 7(h)","v":true},{"t":"Short periods skip weekends in both courts","d":"In the Superior Court and the Supreme Court, a period of 10 days or less excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, unless the rule says “calendar days.” Longer periods count every day.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1)(D); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 26(a)(2)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing deadline is 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time","d":"E-filing is available at all hours. A document is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. ChST, and the date relates back if the Clerk accepts it later. An outage does not extend deadlines. If the system is down more than 48 hours, file in person or by email to the Clerk.\nThe e-filing rules add 1 day to respond after e-service. The civil rules add 3 days after mail and certain other service, and the Supreme Court rules add 5 days only for service not made through the e-filing system. Each e-filing also costs $0.75 on top of the court fee.","c":"NMI R. Elec. Filing 9(d)–(e), 13, 16; NMI R. Civ. P. 6(d); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 26(c); Fee Schedule item 80","v":true},{"t":"Citation rules: local cases, Am. Jur./C.J.S., and unpublished decisions","d":"Supreme Court briefs must cite relevant local cases, and failing to cite binding local authority can bring sanctions. In the Superior Court, citing Am. Jur. or C.J.S. as primary authority is “strictly prohibited” unless a parenthetical explains why no primary authority could be found. Before citing an unpublished Superior Court or Supreme Court decision, give all parties written notice and copies at least 48 hours before the hearing. Attach copies of hard-to-find authority, and the full text of any law review article cited.","c":"Gen. Order 2000-200 (Sup. Ct.); NMI R. Civ. P. 82.2(b)–(e)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Northern Mariana Islands Supreme Court Rules (NMI Sup. Ct. R.), as revised by Errata Order No. 2024-ADM-0006-RUL, effective March 15, 2024 (rules adopted Nov. 12, 2022, No. 2022-ADM-0020-RUL); NMI Rules of Civil Procedure (eff. Jan. 9, 2019, Errata Order 2019-ADM-0003-RUL); NMI Rules for Electronic Filing and Service (eff. Dec. 11, 2021); Judiciary Fee Schedule (eff. Mar. 1, 2017); NMI Const. art. IV; 1 CMC §§ 3102, 3103, 3105. Official texts from the CNMI Law Revision Commission (cnmilaw.gov). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in the CNMI: one appellate court, federal-style rules, Commonwealth deadlines","d":"The NMI Supreme Court is the only Commonwealth appellate court; there is no intermediate court. Its rules follow the Federal Rules of Appellate Procedure in numbering and much of their text, with sections marked “[Reserved]” where the CNMI departs. They also borrow Ninth Circuit practices such as the docketing statement, automatic brief extensions, and attorney-fee requests.\nThe traps come from the trial-court rules that feed into the appeal. The 30-day appeal period applies even when the government is a party. Post-judgment motions that toll the appeal must be filed within the NMI civil rules’ short 10- or 20-day limits. Briefs are capped by pages, not words.\nEverything is filed electronically. Periods of 10 days or less skip weekends and holidays.","c":"NMI Const. art. IV, § 3; 1 CMC §§ 3102, 3105; NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a), 25, 26(a), 32(a)(7)"},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"File the notice of appeal in the Superior Court","d":"An appeal is taken only by filing a notice of appeal with the Superior Court clerk within the Rule 4 deadline. Missing any other step does not void the appeal, but the Court may dismiss for it. A notice filed by mistake in the Supreme Court is sent down and counts as filed on the date received.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(a), 4(d)"},{"t":"Contents: name the appellants and attach the judgment","d":"The notice must name each appealing party, designate and attach the judgment or order appealed, and say whether counsel is appointed. Form 1 is a suggested form. In a civil case the attached judgment must be a separate document (Commonwealth v. Kumagai, 2006 MP 20 ¶ 22, cited in the rule’s note). Informality of form is not grounds for dismissal.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(c)(1), 3(c)(4)–(5) & note 1"},{"t":"Fees","d":"Pay all required fees when you file the notice. The notice-of-appeal fee is $175, paid to the Superior Court, and it also covers interlocutory appeals and civil cross-appeals. Other Supreme Court fees: extraordinary writ $300; petition for rehearing $250; motion to extend brief time $50; motion for late brief $100; motions decided by the full Court $200; emergency motions $200; amicus leave $200. Each e-filing adds $0.75 plus the provider’s charges.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3(e); Judiciary Fee Schedule items 80–81, 85–96"},{"t":"What the Supreme Court can do","d":"The Court may affirm, modify, vacate, or reverse and remand. It may not take new evidence or decide facts de novo, and it may set aside findings of fact only if clearly erroneous, giving due regard to the trial court’s view of witness credibility.","c":"1 CMC § 3103"}]],["Time to appeal",[{"t":"30 days after entry, for everyone","d":"In a civil case, file the notice within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order. Unlike FRAP 4(a)(1)(B), there is no 60-day period when the Commonwealth is a party.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and other parties","d":"Once one party files a timely notice, any other party may file within 14 days after the first notice, or within the regular 30 days if that ends later.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(3)"},{"t":"Early notices","d":"A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. The Superior Court clerk holds it until the separate judgment is filed.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(2)"},{"t":"When a judgment is “entered”","d":"Entry occurs when the judgment is docketed under NMI R. Civ. P. 79(a) and either (1) it is set out in a separate document or (2) 150 days have run since docketing, whichever comes first.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(7)(A)(ii)"},{"t":"Extensions and reopening (Superior Court only)","d":"The Superior Court may extend the time if a party moves within 30 days after the deadline expires and shows excusable neglect or good cause. The extension may not exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later.\nThe Superior Court may reopen the time for 14 days only if: the movant did not receive notice of entry under Civil Rule 77(d) within 21 days; the motion is filed within 180 days of entry, or within 14 days of receiving notice if that is earlier; and no party would be prejudiced. The Supreme Court cannot otherwise extend the time to appeal.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(5)–(6), 26(b)(1); NMI R. Civ. P. 77(d)(2)"}]],["Tolling motions: timely under the NMI civil rules",[{"t":"Which motions restart the clock","d":"The time to appeal runs from the order disposing of the last of these motions, if the motion was timely under the NMI Rules of Civil Procedure:\n• renewed judgment as a matter of law (Rule 50(b), 10 days);\n• amended findings (Rule 52(b), 10 days);\n• attorney fees (Rule 54(d)(2), 14 days);\n• alter or amend (Rule 59(e), 10 days);\n• new trial (Rule 59(b), 20 days);\n• relief from judgment under Rule 60, if filed within 28 days after judgment.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A); NMI R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 54(d)(2)(B)(i), 59(b), 59(e)"},{"t":"No extensions of the motion deadlines","d":"The Superior Court may not extend the time for Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), or (e), or 60(b) motions. Periods of 10 days or less skip intermediate weekends and holidays.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 6(a)(1)(D), 6(b)(2)"},{"t":"Challenging the ruling on the motion","d":"A notice of appeal filed while a tolling motion is pending takes effect when the last such motion is decided. To challenge the order on the motion, or a judgment amended because of it, file a new or amended notice within the Rule 4 time measured from that order.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(B)(i)–(ii)"}]],["Docketing, transcripts, and the record",[{"t":"Docketing statement (Form 2): 15 days","d":"Every represented appellant must file a docketing statement within 15 days after the Clerk gives notice that the appeal is docketed. Extensions are disfavored and require substantial need. Failing to file is grounds for dismissal, and an inaccurate statement can be sanctioned. Within 10 days after service, each opposing party must file a response or a notice of appearance. Raise jurisdictional defects by motion to dismiss, not in the response.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Conflicts notice and representation statement","d":"With the first docketing statement, response, or appearance, file a notice of potential conflicts under 1 CMC § 3308 that might affect a justice. Within 15 days after filing the notice of appeal, appellant’s counsel files a representation statement, which may be part of the docketing statement. Any change in representation must be noticed within 30 days.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(c), 12(b)–(c)"},{"t":"Ordering transcripts (Form 3): 15 days","d":"Within 15 days after the notice of appeal, or after the order deciding the last tolling motion if later, the appellant files Form 3 with the Superior Court clerk. Form 3 either orders audio transcript portions or certifies that no transcript will be ordered. The appellee may add portions within 10 days, and the appellant may respond within 5. The clerk prepares audio transcripts within 10 days.\nCertified transcripts are designated on Form 4 within 15 days after the clerk’s completion notice and are due within 30 days. Audio transcripts may go into the record only by motion. They are allowed in criminal appeals on a showing of need and disfavored in civil cases. If you argue a finding lacks evidentiary support, the record must include all the relevant evidence.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(a)–(d)"},{"t":"Certification of the record","d":"The Superior Court clerk keeps the record and certifies it as complete. Certification comes within 20 days after the appellant certifies that no transcript will be ordered, or within 10 days after the certified transcript is filed. The record is filed in the Supreme Court when the certificate is filed, and that date starts the briefing clock.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 10(a), 11(c)(2)–(3)"},{"t":"Appendix","d":"The appellant files one appendix with the opening brief and pays for it, including parts the appellee designates. Absent agreement, the appellant serves designations within 10 days after the record is filed, and the appellee counter-designates within 10 days. Trial-court memoranda generally do not belong in it, and you cannot incorporate trial briefs by reference. Padding the appendix can be sanctioned.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 30(a)–(b), (d)"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Schedule: 40 / 30 / 14","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the record is filed. The appellee’s brief is due 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. The reply is due 14 days after the appellee’s brief, and at least 3 days before argument. If the appellant misses the deadline, the appellee may move to dismiss. An appellee who files no brief is not heard at argument without permission.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a), 31(c)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"The first party to file a notice is the appellant; if notices are filed the same day, it is the plaintiff below. There are four briefs, served at 40 days after the record is filed, then 30, 30, and 14 days after each preceding brief. The last brief is due at least 3 days before argument.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 28.1(b), (f)"},{"t":"Extensions: one automatic 30-day extension per brief","d":"A party may get one automatic extension of up to 30 days per brief by a motion citing Rule 31-1(a)(1), filed before the brief is due; the Clerk grants it. Any further extension requires a motion with an affidavit showing diligence and substantial need. Late briefs need leave and are highly disfavored.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31-1(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Length and form","d":"A principal brief may not exceed 35 pages, and a reply 20 pages, counting everything including the caption, headings, and tables. Format: 11-point Times New Roman, 1.5 spacing, 1-inch margins, a white cover, and a certificate of compliance. Every paper must show the attorney’s CNMI Bar number. An amicus brief may be half the length of a principal brief and is due 7 days after the supported party’s principal brief. A brief that does not conform may be struck, fined, or, for an appellant, lead to dismissal.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 29(d)–(e), 32(a)(2)–(7), 32(d), 32(g)–(i)"},{"t":"Contents","d":"A jurisdictional statement must show the basis for the Superior Court’s and the Supreme Court’s jurisdiction, the filing dates proving timeliness, and that the appeal is from a final judgment entered as a separate document, or another basis for jurisdiction. State the standard of review for each issue. A corporate disclosure statement goes before the table of contents. Supplemental authority letters are limited to 350 words.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 26.1, 28(a)(4), 28(a)(8)(B), 28(j)"},{"t":"Citations","d":"Unpublished dispositions may be cited, though this is highly disfavored, if flagged as unpublished. File copies of any authority not in a public electronic database. A standing order requires citation to relevant local case law, with sanctions possible for omitting binding local authority.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 32.1; Gen. Order 2000-200"}]],["Motions and stays",[{"t":"Motion practice","d":"Responses to motions are due in 8 days and replies in 5; both are counted excluding weekends because they are under 10 days. Motions and responses are limited to 20 pages and replies to 10. A separate supporting brief is not allowed. Each motion needs a proposed order, and a motion for substantive relief must attach the order below. The Clerk may decide routine unopposed motions and brief extensions, and a single justice may decide most others.\nTo seek reconsideration of an order on a motion, move within 10 days after service of the order.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27(a)(3)–(5), 27(d)(2), 27-2(a)–(d)"},{"t":"Emergency motions","d":"If relief is needed within 21 days to avoid irreparable harm, label the motion “Emergency Motion Under Rule 27-1.” Include a Rule 27-1 Certificate with contact details for all counsel, the facts of the emergency, and how and when opponents were notified. Notify the Clerk and opponents in advance.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27-1"},{"t":"Stays and supersedeas bonds","d":"Move first in the Superior Court unless that would be impracticable. Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry, but injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. A supersedeas bond for a money judgment is the judgment plus 10% plus $250, unless justice requires otherwise. The Commonwealth needs no bond.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 8(a); NMI R. Civ. P. 62(a), (d)–(e)"}]],["Argument, decision, and rehearing",[{"t":"Oral argument","d":"Argument is allowed unless all three panel justices agree it is unnecessary: the appeal is frivolous, the issue has been authoritatively decided, or the briefs are enough. Each side gets 25 minutes. Leave to use physical exhibits must be sought at least one week before argument.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 34(a)–(b), (g)"},{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days, 15 pages, no answer unless requested","d":"A petition for rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment. It must state with particularity the points of law or fact overlooked or misapprehended. It is limited to 15 pages, and no oral argument is allowed. No answer is permitted unless the Court requests one, and rehearing is ordinarily not granted without such a request. The fee is $250.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 40; Fee Schedule item 87"},{"t":"Mandate, costs, and attorney fees","d":"The mandate issues 7 calendar days after the rehearing period ends, or 7 days after denial of a timely rehearing petition, stay motion, or fee petition, whichever is later. File a bill of costs within 14 days after entry of judgment; objections are due within 10 days. File an attorney-fee request no later than 5 calendar days after the rehearing period expires, or 5 days after a rehearing petition is decided. Oppositions to a fee request are due in 10 days and replies in 5. For frivolous appeals the Court may award damages and single or double costs.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 38, 39(d), 39-1, 41(b)"}]],["Interlocutory review and writs",[{"t":"No general permissive-appeal rule","d":"Rules 5 and 6 are reserved, so there is no FRAP 5–style petition for permission to appeal an interlocutory order. The Constitution gives the Supreme Court appeals from “final judgments and orders.” The statute speaks of “judgments and orders” of the Superior Court. Whether a particular non-final order is appealable, for example under the collateral-order doctrine, depends on case law not collected here.","c":"NMI Const. art. IV, § 3; 1 CMC § 3102(a); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 5–6 (reserved)"},{"t":"Built-in interlocutory routes","d":"Class certification: the Supreme Court may permit an appeal if a petition is filed with the Supreme Court clerk within 14 days after the certification order. The appeal does not stay the case unless ordered. Injunctions: the civil rules contemplate appeals from interlocutory orders granting, dissolving, or denying an injunction, and the fee schedule covers interlocutory appeals.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 23(f), 62(c); Fee Schedule item 85"},{"t":"Mandamus and prohibition","d":"Petitions are captioned “In re [petitioner].” All parties below are respondents, but the trial judge is not. The petitioner must declare that the petition was given to all parties and the judge. The petition is limited to 30 pages. The Court may deny without an answer, and writ proceedings take precedence over ordinary civil cases.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 21(a)–(d); 1 CMC § 3102(b)"}]],["Review beyond the NMI Supreme Court; certified questions",[{"t":"Further review","d":"Supreme Court decisions are final and may not be appealed to any other court except as Covenant § 403(a) provides. Section 403(a) applies to Commonwealth courts the federal rules that govern relations between U.S. courts and state courts. Rule 41 provides for staying the mandate while a party seeks certiorari in the U.S. Supreme Court. The stay is limited to 90 days unless the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within that time, in which case it lasts until final disposition. The federal statute governing certiorari was not collected.","c":"1 CMC § 3103 & Commission comment; NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(d)(2)"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"A federal court may certify a question of Commonwealth law that may be determinative where there is no controlling NMI Supreme Court precedent. The Court may accept or refuse it at its sole discretion. Elected or governor-appointed Commonwealth officials in a dispute over their powers may certify legal questions on stipulated facts, and the ruling binds the parties.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 13–14; NMI Const. art. IV, § 11"}]],["E-filing and computing time",[{"t":"Everything is e-filed","d":"Supreme Court filing and service follow the NMI Rules for Electronic Filing and Service, which apply to all CNMI courts. Attorneys, including pro hac vice counsel, must be registered filers. A filing is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time (ChST). If the system is unavailable for more than 48 hours, file in person or by email to the Clerk.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 25(a), 25(c); NMI R. Elec. Filing 1(b), 4, 9(d), 13(c)"},{"t":"Counting days","d":"Periods of 10 days or less exclude intermediate weekends and legal holidays. Legal holidays include any day the Court is closed for any reason, including austerity or weather closures. Longer periods are calendar days. Five calendar days are added after service only when service is not made through the e-filing system. Separately, the e-filing rules add 1 day to respond after e-service.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 1-1(c)(6), 26(a), 26(c); NMI R. Elec. Filing 16"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order (separate document, or 150 days after docketing).\nFile with the Superior Court clerk. No 60-day period for government cases. Restarts after timely tolling motions.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1), 4(a)(7)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"File with the Superior Court clerk. No 60-day period for government cases. Restarts after timely tolling motions."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal.\nOr within the regular 30 days if that ends later.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":"Or within the regular 30 days if that ends later."}},{"t":"Motion to extend time to appeal","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nFiled in the Superior Court; requires excusable neglect or good cause. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(5)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal deadline)","note":"Filed in the Superior Court; requires excusable neglect or good cause. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Motion to reopen time to appeal","d":"180 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nOr 14 days after receiving notice of entry, whichever is earlier. Available only if Rule 77(d) notice was not received within 21 days. Reopens for 14 days.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(6)","due":{"n":180,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Or 14 days after receiving notice of entry, whichever is earlier. Available only if Rule 77(d) notice was not received within 21 days. Reopens for 14 days."}},{"t":"Renewed JMOL (tolls appeal)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment (or jury discharge if issue undecided).\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Weekends excluded (10 days or less).","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 50(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(i)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Federal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Weekends excluded (10 days or less)."}},{"t":"Motion to amend findings (tolls appeal)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 52(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(ii)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Federal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended."}},{"t":"Motion to alter or amend judgment (tolls appeal)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Limited to intervening change in law, new evidence, or clear error or manifest injustice.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 59(e); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(iv)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Federal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Limited to intervening change in law, new evidence, or clear error or manifest injustice."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial (tolls appeal)","d":"20 days from entry of judgment.\nFederal rule allows 28 days. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. Cannot be extended.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 59(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(v)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Federal rule allows 28 days. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. Cannot be extended."}},{"t":"Attorney-fee motion in Superior Court (tolls appeal)","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nUnless a statute or court order provides otherwise.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)(B)(i); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(iii)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Unless a statute or court order provides otherwise."}},{"t":"Rule 60 motion that tolls the appeal","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nA Rule 60(b) motion may be filed later (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3), but then it does not toll.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(vi)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"A Rule 60(b) motion may be filed later (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3), but then it does not toll."}},{"t":"Docketing statement (Form 2)","d":"15 days from clerk’s notice that the appeal is docketed.\nExtensions disfavored; failure is grounds for dismissal. Not required of pro se appellants.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(a)(4)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice that the appeal is docketed)","note":"Extensions disfavored; failure is grounds for dismissal. Not required of pro se appellants."}},{"t":"Response to docketing statement or notice of appearance","d":"10 days from service of the docketing statement.\nFile the conflicts notice at the same time (Rule 3-1(c)). Counted excluding weekends (10 days or less).","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the docketing statement)","note":"File the conflicts notice at the same time (Rule 3-1(c)). Counted excluding weekends (10 days or less)."}},{"t":"Representation statement","d":"15 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nMay be included in the docketing statement.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 12(b)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"May be included in the docketing statement."}},{"t":"Transcript order or no-transcript certification (Form 3)","d":"15 days from notice of appeal, or order deciding last tolling motion, if later.\nAppellee may add portions within 10 days; appellant may respond within 5 days.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(b)(1)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (notice of appeal, or order deciding last tolling motion, if later)","note":"Appellee may add portions within 10 days; appellant may respond within 5 days."}},{"t":"Certified transcript designation (Form 4)","d":"15 days from service of the Rule 11-1(b)(4) completion notice.\nJoint or separate. Certified transcript is due within 30 days after Form 4 unless more time is granted.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(c)(1), 11-1(d)(1)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the Rule 11-1(b)(4) completion notice)","note":"Joint or separate. Certified transcript is due within 30 days after Form 4 unless more time is granted."}},{"t":"Appellant’s opening brief and appendix","d":"40 days from filing of the certificate of record.\n35-page limit, inclusive of everything. One automatic extension of up to 30 days is available.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 30(a)(3), 31(a)(1)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the certificate of record)","note":"35-page limit, inclusive of everything. One automatic extension of up to 30 days is available."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n35-page limit.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"35-page limit."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nMust be filed at least 3 days before argument. 20-page limit.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"Must be filed at least 3 days before argument. 20-page limit."}},{"t":"Amicus brief","d":"7 days from filing of the supported party’s principal brief.\nHalf the principal-brief length. Leave or consent needed, except for the Commonwealth.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 29(e)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the supported party’s principal brief)","note":"Half the principal-brief length. Leave or consent needed, except for the Commonwealth."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"8 days from service of the motion.\nReply is due within 5 days after the response. Both are counted excluding weekends and holidays.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27(a)(4)(A)","due":{"n":8,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Reply is due within 5 days after the response. Both are counted excluding weekends and holidays."}},{"t":"Motion to reconsider an order on a motion","d":"10 days from service of the order.\nResponse is due within 7 days for orders by the Clerk or a single justice.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27-2(d)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the order)","note":"Response is due within 7 days for orders by the Clerk or a single justice."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal class-certification order","d":"14 days from entry of the order.\nFiled with the Supreme Court clerk; does not stay proceedings unless ordered.","c":"NMI R. Civ. P. 23(f)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Filed with the Supreme Court clerk; does not stay proceedings unless ordered."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\n15 pages; no answer unless requested; $250 fee.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 40(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"15 pages; no answer unless requested; $250 fee."}},{"t":"Bill of costs","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nObjections are due within 10 days after service.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 39(d)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Objections are due within 10 days after service."}},{"t":"Request for appellate attorney fees","d":"5 days from expiration of the rehearing period (or disposition of a timely rehearing petition).\nCalendar days. File separately from the bill of costs. Opposition due in 10 days; reply in 5.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 39-1(a)(1)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the rehearing period)","note":"Calendar days. File separately from the bill of costs. Opposition due in 10 days; reply in 5."}},{"t":"Issuance of mandate","d":"7 days from expiration of rehearing period, or denial of rehearing, stay motion, or fee petition (whichever is later).\nCalendar days. A timely rehearing petition, stay motion, or fee petition stays the mandate.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(b)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of rehearing period, or denial of rehearing, stay motion, or fee petition)","note":"Calendar days. A timely rehearing petition, stay motion, or fee petition stays the mandate."}},{"t":"Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari","d":"90 days from grant of the stay.\nContinues until final disposition if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay period.","c":"NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(d)(2)(B)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (grant of the stay)","note":"Continues until final disposition if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay period."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (civil)",30,"Entry of the judgment or order (separate document, or 150 days after docketing)","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(1), 4(a)(7)","File with the Superior Court clerk. No 60-day period for government cases. Restarts after timely tolling motions."],["Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)",14,"Filing of the first notice of appeal","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(3)","Or within the regular 30 days if that ends later."],["Motion to extend time to appeal",30,"Expiration of the original appeal deadline","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(5)","Filed in the Superior Court; requires excusable neglect or good cause. The extension cannot exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later."],["Motion to reopen time to appeal",180,"Entry of the judgment or order","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(6)","Or 14 days after receiving notice of entry, whichever is earlier. Available only if Rule 77(d) notice was not received within 21 days. Reopens for 14 days."],["Renewed JMOL (tolls appeal)",10,"Entry of judgment (or jury discharge if issue undecided)","NMI R. Civ. P. 50(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(i)","Federal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Weekends excluded (10 days or less)."],["Motion to amend findings (tolls appeal)",10,"Entry of judgment","NMI R. Civ. P. 52(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(ii)","Federal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended."],["Motion to alter or amend judgment (tolls appeal)",10,"Entry of judgment","NMI R. Civ. P. 59(e); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(iv)","Federal rule allows 28 days. Cannot be extended. Limited to intervening change in law, new evidence, or clear error or manifest injustice."],["Motion for new trial (tolls appeal)",20,"Entry of judgment","NMI R. Civ. P. 59(b); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(v)","Federal rule allows 28 days. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service. Cannot be extended."],["Attorney-fee motion in Superior Court (tolls appeal)",14,"Entry of judgment","NMI R. Civ. P. 54(d)(2)(B)(i); NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(iii)","Unless a statute or court order provides otherwise."],["Rule 60 motion that tolls the appeal",28,"Entry of judgment","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(4)(A)(vi)","A Rule 60(b) motion may be filed later (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3), but then it does not toll."],["Docketing statement (Form 2)",15,"Clerk’s notice that the appeal is docketed","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(a)(4)","Extensions disfavored; failure is grounds for dismissal. Not required of pro se appellants."],["Response to docketing statement or notice of appearance",10,"Service of the docketing statement","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 3-1(b)","File the conflicts notice at the same time (Rule 3-1(c)). Counted excluding weekends (10 days or less)."],["Representation statement",15,"Filing of the notice of appeal","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 12(b)","May be included in the docketing statement."],["Transcript order or no-transcript certification (Form 3)",15,"Notice of appeal, or order deciding last tolling motion, if later","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(b)(1)","Appellee may add portions within 10 days; appellant may respond within 5 days."],["Certified transcript designation (Form 4)",15,"Service of the Rule 11-1(b)(4) completion notice","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 11-1(c)(1), 11-1(d)(1)","Joint or separate. Certified transcript is due within 30 days after Form 4 unless more time is granted."],["Appellant’s opening brief and appendix",40,"Filing of the certificate of record","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 30(a)(3), 31(a)(1)","35-page limit, inclusive of everything. One automatic extension of up to 30 days is available."],["Appellee’s brief",30,"Service of the appellant’s brief","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a)(1)","35-page limit."],["Reply brief",14,"Service of the appellee’s brief","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 31(a)(1)","Must be filed at least 3 days before argument. 20-page limit."],["Amicus brief",7,"Filing of the supported party’s principal brief","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 29(e)","Half the principal-brief length. Leave or consent needed, except for the Commonwealth."],["Response to a motion",8,"Service of the motion","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27(a)(4)(A)","Reply is due within 5 days after the response. Both are counted excluding weekends and holidays."],["Motion to reconsider an order on a motion",10,"Service of the order","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 27-2(d)(1)","Response is due within 7 days for orders by the Clerk or a single justice."],["Petition for permission to appeal class-certification order",14,"Entry of the order","NMI R. Civ. P. 23(f)","Filed with the Supreme Court clerk; does not stay proceedings unless ordered."],["Petition for rehearing",14,"Entry of judgment","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 40(a)(1)","15 pages; no answer unless requested; $250 fee."],["Bill of costs",14,"Entry of judgment","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 39(d)(1)","Objections are due within 10 days after service."],["Request for appellate attorney fees",5,"Expiration of the rehearing period (or disposition of a timely rehearing petition)","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 39-1(a)(1)","Calendar days. File separately from the bill of costs. Opposition due in 10 days; reply in 5."],["Issuance of mandate",7,"Expiration of rehearing period, or denial of rehearing, stay motion, or fee petition (whichever is later)","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(b)","Calendar days. A timely rehearing petition, stay motion, or fee petition stays the mandate."],["Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari",90,"Grant of the stay","NMI Sup. Ct. R. 41(d)(2)(B)","Continues until final disposition if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay period."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.nmijudiciary.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Northern Mariana Islands Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope and purpose; definitions","s":"Governs all civil suits in the Superior Court, at law or in equity, construed for just, speedy, and inexpensive determination. Applies to actions filed after the January 9, 2019 effective date. Defines “address” as physical and mailing address; a Commonwealth physical address must be described well enough to identify the property.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action: the civil action.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencing an action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint with the Superior Court.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons","s":"Mirrors FRCP 4 with Commonwealth changes. A request to waive service must allow 30 days to return it, or 60 days if the defendant is outside the Commonwealth. A defendant who waives has 60 days to answer, or 90 days if outside the Commonwealth. Serve a corporation through an officer or agent, or the resident agent shown on its last annual report to the Registrar. Serve the Commonwealth by delivery to the Attorney General’s office or by registered or certified mail to the Attorney General. The service deadline is 120 days (federal: 90). Dismissal for late service is barred once the defendant is served, and extensions must be freely given.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","s":"Service on an attorney may be by mail to the attorney’s registered CNMI Bar or other state-bar address. Mail service is not allowed for papers about a motion set for hearing, or a matter set for trial, within 10 days of that date. Off-island counsel must associate local counsel who keeps a Commonwealth office and takes a meaningful part in the case, and service on local counsel is effective (Rule 5(b)(4)). Discovery requests and responses are not filed, and originals must be kept until 6 months after final disposition. Filing is through the NMI e-filing rules unless the judge accepts a filing.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Does not track the federal rule. Periods of 10 days or less exclude intermediate weekends and legal holidays unless stated in calendar days. Courts may not extend the times under Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), and 60(b). A pre-judgment motion must be filed and served at least 30 days before the hearing; the opposition is due 9 days after service and the reply 6 days after the opposition. For post-judgment motions the periods are 14, 7, and 2 calendar days. A motion to shorten time needs a separate motion with a declaration, and the court should allow 2 days to oppose it. Add 3 days after service by mail, by leaving with the clerk, or by other Rule 5(b) means.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions; time limits","s":"Pleadings as in FRCP 7(a). A memorandum is required when a motion turns on interpretation of law. Memoranda are limited to 25 pages and replies to 15, and the court may strike excess pages or the whole memorandum. The clerk, not counsel, sets the hearing date and time on the notice of hearing. The court may rule without a hearing after giving 2 days to object. Papers must be 11-point Times New Roman or another serif font, double-spaced, with a set first-page format that includes the attorney’s bar number. Oral argument is limited to 15 minutes for the movant, 15 for the opponent, and 5 for reply.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Disclosure statement","s":"A nongovernmental corporate party must file 2 copies of a statement identifying any parent corporation and any public company owning 10% or more of its stock, or stating there is none. File it with the first appearance or filing, and supplement it promptly if the information changes.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Mirrors FRCP 8: a short and plain statement of the claim and relief, admissions and denials, and affirmative defenses (the listed defenses include the statute of frauds).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Mirrors FRCP 9. Raise capacity by specific denial, plead fraud or mistake with particularity, and specifically plead special damages.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number, and the Rule 7(e) designation, and the complaint must name all parties. Use numbered paragraphs, with a separate count for each transaction where that helps clarity. A written-instrument exhibit is part of the pleading. The rule does not track the federal text.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing papers; representations; sanctions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 11, including the 21-day safe harbor to withdraw or correct a challenged paper. Papers must also list an email address and be filed and signed under the e-filing rules.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motions","s":"An answer is due 21 days after service, or 60 days after a waiver request is sent (90 days if sent outside any U.S. jurisdiction). The Commonwealth, its agencies, and officers sued officially get 30 days after service on the Attorney General (federal: 60 days). Officers sued individually get 30 days after the later of service on them or on the Attorney General. After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due in 14 days, or 14 days after a more definite statement is served. Ignoring an order for a more definite statement for 14 days risks striking. A motion to strike is due within 21 days of service when no response is allowed (the rule’s note says 20). Rule 12(b) defenses and waiver otherwise follow the federal rule.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Mirrors FRCP 13 on compulsory and permissive counterclaims and crossclaims.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"Mirrors FRCP 14. A defendant needs leave to file a third-party complaint more than 14 days after serving its original answer.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"Mirrors FRCP 15. Amendment once as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after a responsive pleading or Rule 12 motion; otherwise with written consent or leave. A response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining time or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back follows the federal rule.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"Does not track the federal text. The clerk must set a mandatory scheduling conference within 90 days after the complaint is filed, or within 45 days after the last permitted pleading, whichever is later. The scheduling order limits time to join parties, amend, file motions, and complete discovery, and changes need good cause. The final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. For noncompliance, an award of expenses is mandatory unless substantially justified, along with Rule 37(b) sanctions.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Plaintiff and defendant; capacity; public officers","s":"Mirrors FRCP 17 on real party in interest, capacity, and minors or incompetent persons.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"Mirrors FRCP 18: a party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"Mirrors FRCP 19, subject to Rule 23.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Mirrors FRCP 20.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Mirrors FRCP 21. Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add, drop, or sever parties and claims.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Mirrors FRCP 22.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 23, including settlement approval and class-counsel fees. The NMI Supreme Court may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying certification if a petition is filed with the Supreme Court clerk within 14 days after the order. The appeal does not stay the case unless ordered.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 23.1.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Mirrors FRCP 23.2.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Mirrors FRCP 24 on intervention of right and permissive intervention.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"Mirrors FRCP 25. After death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after service of a statement noting the death, or the action against the decedent must be dismissed.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"Duty to disclose; general discovery provisions","s":"Largely mirrors FRCP 26, including proportionality. Initial disclosures are due within 14 days after the Rule 26(f) conference, or 30 days after a later party is served or joined. Expert disclosures are due at least 90 days before trial, and rebuttal within 30 days after the other side’s. Pretrial disclosures are due at least 30 days before trial, with objections within 14 days. Rule 34 requests may be delivered early, more than 21 days after service. The parties confer at least 21 days before the scheduling conference and file the discovery plan within 14 days after conferring. The list of exempt proceedings includes domestic-violence protection-order actions.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"Mirrors FRCP 27. Serve the petition and notice at least 21 days before the hearing.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Mirrors FRCP 28.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Mirrors FRCP 29.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Mirrors FRCP 30, with a 10-deposition limit per side and one day of 7 hours. The deponent has 30 days to review the transcript. Commonwealth additions retained from the former rule: a party who lived or had its principal place of business in the Commonwealth when the action began must be deposed in the Commonwealth unless the parties stipulate otherwise, including by videoconference. Other parties and non-parties are deposed where they live, and each party bears its own costs of attending. Counsel has 30 days after the case ends to withdraw filed transcripts before the clerk may destroy them.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 31. Cross-questions are due 14 days after service, redirect 7 days after cross, and recross 7 days after redirect.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court proceedings","s":"Mirrors FRCP 32. A deposition taken on less than 14 days’ notice may not be used against a party who promptly moved for a protective order. Objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within 7 days.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories","s":"Limit of 25, including discrete subparts. Answers and objections are due in 30 days. Grounds for objection must be stated with specificity or are waived, and unobjected parts must be answered. If the court orders answers, they are due within 15 days unless the court directs otherwise (an NMI addition).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","s":"Mirrors FRCP 34. Respond in writing within 30 days after service, or 30 days after the first Rule 26(f) conference for early requests.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"Mirrors FRCP 35.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Mirrors FRCP 36: matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days. NMI addition: if an objection is later overruled, the matter is deemed admitted unless the party serves a denial or explanation within 10 days after that ruling, or another period the court sets.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to disclose or cooperate; sanctions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 37, including the meet-and-confer certification, the expense-shifting rules, and the ESI-loss provision. Measures to cure prejudice are allowed; adverse-inference instructions or dismissal require intent to deprive. There are no sanctions for ESI lost through routine good-faith system operation.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"Serve a written jury demand no later than 60 days after the last pleading is filed, file it, and pay the jury fee on or before the filing date; otherwise the jury is waived. Other parties may demand additional issues within 14 days. A proper demand may be withdrawn only with the parties’ consent and no later than 45 days before trial. Jury fees are non-refundable ($400 under the 2017 fee schedule).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Mirrors FRCP 39, with Commonwealth law determining the right to a jury. A consent jury is not available against the Commonwealth where a statute provides for nonjury trial.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Scheduling cases for trial","s":"Does not track the federal rule; parts follow a Kansas statute. Statutory priorities come first, and continuances are for good cause. A case on file 6 months is “at issue,” and a party may file an at-issue memorandum; an opponent who disagrees must respond within 10 days. A continuance for an absent witness or evidence needs an affidavit or declaration with listed contents. Scheduling stipulations must be in writing unless made in open court. Notify the court promptly of any settlement.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 41. The two-dismissal rule counts prior Commonwealth, federal, state, and territorial actions. An action may also be dismissed without prejudice for lack of prosecution under Rule 16 of the Commonwealth Rules of Practice.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Mirrors FRCP 42.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Mirrors FRCP 43.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proving an official record","s":"Mirrors FRCP 44, adapted to records kept in the Commonwealth.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determining foreign law","s":"Give notice by pleading or other writing. The court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling on law.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Mirrors FRCP 45 with Commonwealth changes. A subpoena may be served anywhere in the Commonwealth, and outside it only with the court’s authorization on motion. If attendance is required on a different island from where service was made, a round-trip airline ticket must be tendered. Failing to travel to the Commonwealth is excused unless airfare was provided in advance. Objections to production are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"45.1","t":"Notice to appear in lieu of subpoena","s":"A written notice served on a party’s attorney at least 10 days before the appearance compels a party, or its officer, director, or managing agent, to attend as if subpoenaed. With at least 20 days’ notice it may require them to bring specified documents. This is an NMI rule with no FRCP counterpart.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling or order","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; state the request or objection and the grounds.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Selection of jurors","s":"Peremptory challenges are set by 7 CMC § 3102. The court holds a pretrial jury-selection hearing where counsel may stipulate to an English-speaking jury and strike by agreement names that are clearly disqualified, for example by relationship or by 7 CMC §§ 3103, 3104, or 3111. Challenges at trial are unaffected.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; verdict; polling","s":"The verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of 6. The jury is polled on request.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdict; general verdict and questions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 49.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law","s":"Mirrors FRCP 50, but a renewed motion, with or without an alternative new-trial request, is due within 10 days after entry of judgment, or 10 days after the jury is discharged if the issue went undecided (federal: 28). A losing party’s new-trial motion after a JMOL is due within 10 days.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Jury instructions; objections","s":"Mirrors FRCP 51, including plain-error review of unpreserved instruction errors that affect substantial rights.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court; partial findings","s":"Mirrors FRCP 52, with clear-error review of findings. A motion to amend or add findings is due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Mirrors FRCP 53, but objections to, or motions to adopt or modify, a master’s order or report are due within 10 days after service unless the court sets another time.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgment; costs","s":"Mirrors FRCP 54. The clerk taxes costs on 7 days’ notice, and the court reviews on a motion served within the next 7 days. Costs against the Commonwealth are allowed only as permitted by law. An attorney-fee motion is due within 14 days after entry of judgment.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"Mirrors FRCP 55. A party who has appeared gets written notice of a default-judgment application at least 7 days before the hearing. A default judgment against the Commonwealth requires proof that satisfies the court.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Mirrors FRCP 56. Unless ordered otherwise, a motion may be filed until 30 days after the close of all discovery. Supporting or opposing evidence over 25 pages must include a table of contents (an NMI addition).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgment","s":"Mirrors FRCP 57.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entering judgments","s":"Does not track FRCP 58. Within 10 days after a decision that needs a settled judgment or order, the prevailing party serves a draft and delivers it to the clerk. Others have 5 days to approve or object with a substitute draft, and at 15 days the clerk submits everything to the judge. The same procedure applies to findings and conclusions after a bench trial. An initialed approval is enough for entry.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; altering or amending a judgment","s":"A new-trial motion is due within 20 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28), and opposing affidavits within 14 days. The court may order a new trial on its own within 20 days. A Rule 59(e) motion is due within 10 days (federal: 28) and is limited to an intervening change in law, new evidence, or clear error or manifest injustice. Rule 11 sanctions expressly apply. None of these times can be extended.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from a judgment or order","s":"Mirrors FRCP 60, but relief from a void judgment is stated separately and is mandatory. Motions for reasons (1)–(3) must be made within one year, and others within a reasonable time. Clerical corrections need Supreme Court leave once an appeal is docketed.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry, but injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. A supersedeas bond for a money judgment is the judgment plus 10% plus $250 for costs unless justice requires otherwise, which retains the former Commonwealth formula. The Commonwealth needs no bond. The Supreme Court’s power to stay is not limited.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"62.1","t":"Indicative ruling while an appeal is pending","s":"Mirrors FRCP 62.1. The court may defer, deny, or state that it would grant the motion or that it raises a substantial issue, and the movant must then notify the Supreme Court. This is new to the Commonwealth rules.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judge to proceed","s":"A successor judge may continue after certifying familiarity with the record. In a bench trial, the successor must recall a material, disputed, available witness on request.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of property","s":"Prejudgment seizure remedies are available as provided by Commonwealth law.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions and restraining orders","s":"Mirrors FRCP 65, but a TRO without notice expires within the time the court sets, not to exceed 10 days (federal: 14), unless extended for a like period or by consent. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. The Commonwealth and its instrumentalities need not give security.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Proceedings against a surety","s":"Mirrors FRCP 65.1.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which the court has appointed a receiver may not be dismissed without court order, and these rules govern receivership actions.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit into court","s":"Mirrors FRCP 67. Deposits are made by leave on notice and held in interest-bearing accounts or instruments.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"Does not track FRCP 68. A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial, and acceptance must be served within 10 days. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn. After liability is fixed, an offer may be made at least 10 days before the damages hearing. An offeree who does no better than the offer pays the costs incurred after it.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by any means Commonwealth law authorizes, under the law in force when the remedy is sought. The judgment creditor may take discovery from anyone, including the debtor.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"Mirrors FRCP 70.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","s":"Mirrors FRCP 71.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"71.1","t":"Condemnation of property","s":"Does not track FRCP 71.1. Filing follows the e-filing rules, and defendants answer within 20 days after service of the notice.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"71.2","t":"Habeas corpus cases","s":"Habeas proceedings follow Commonwealth statutes and must be consistent with these rules (formerly Rule 72).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"73","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved. A former Rule 73 took effect July 6, 2009 and expired July 6, 2014.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"74","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"75","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"76","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"Court and clerks","s":"The court is always open for e-filing. Hearings other than ex parte ones may be held outside the Commonwealth only if all affected parties consent. The clerk may enter some consent orders, including one 10-day extension to plead or move (not for a new-trial motion) if the time has not run. Lack of notice of entry does not affect the time to appeal except as the Supreme Court Rules allow.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Hearing motions; submission on briefs","s":"The court may set regular motion days and may decide motions on the papers.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps the civil docket, judgments, indexes, and calendars. Jury cases are marked “jury.” Entry in the docket under Rule 79(a) triggers appeal time under NMI Sup. Ct. R. 4(a)(7).","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Transcript as evidence","s":"When former testimony is admissible, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the person who recorded or reported it.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability in general","s":"The rules apply to all civil proceedings unless law or another procedural rule provides otherwise. The writs of scire facias and mandamus are abolished in favor of action or motion.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"82.1","t":"Absence of counsel","s":"An attorney who will be away from the Commonwealth, or unavailable at a regular Commonwealth office, for 3 or more consecutive days must file an Off-Island Report on the CNMI Bar Association website at least 15 days before the absence. This is an NMI-only rule.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"82.2","t":"Citation of authority","s":"Commonwealth Reporter and NMI Reporter cases, slip opinions, cases on the Law Revision Commission website, and some unpublished U.S. District Court for the NMI and Ninth Circuit decisions may be cited. To cite an unpublished Superior Court or Supreme Court decision, give written notice and copies at least 48 hours before the hearing. Am. Jur. and C.J.S. may not be cited as primary authority without a parenthetical explaining why no primary authority exists. Attach hard-to-find authority, and provide full law review articles.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved. Former Commonwealth Rule 83 on small claims stays in effect until Small Claims Rules are adopted.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"Court forms are recommended but optional, except that small-claims forms adopted by the court are mandatory.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"[Reserved]","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://cnmilaw.gov/docs/courtrules/R05.pdf"}]});
